---
/*
  TWO PICTURES, ONE SCREEN, NO GUTTER (2026-08-12, section 114).

  THE ONE DEVICE THE REFERENCES USE THAT THIS SITE HAD NEVER BUILT. Rick Owens'
  home page divides a screen vertically and lets each half be a whole picture at
  full height — a portrait against white beside a portrait in acid green, one
  word on each (EYEWEAR, BAGS). It appears four times in the fifty screenshots
  and it is the most recognisable thing about that page after the full-bleed
  hero.

  Everything this site had was a COLUMN: full-bleed frame, then text, then
  full-bleed frame. A reader met one thing at a time all the way down. This is
  the first screen where two photographs argue with each other, and that is the
  whole point of it — the eye compares rather than receives.

  WHY IT WORKS WITH OUR MATERIAL WHEN A FOUR-ACROSS GRID DOES NOT. Section 107's
  finding stands: our frames are concrete, forest, night and studio, so a dense
  grid of them reads as a contact sheet. TWO is not a grid. Two frames chosen to
  share a ground read as one composition, and choosing two is something this
  dataset can actually do — choosing thirty is not.

  NO GUTTER, NO MARGIN, NO ROUNDING. It is two full-bleed surfaces meeting at a
  seam, which is standing rule 9 and is also what the reference does. The seam
  is the composition; a gap would make them two cards.

  ON A PHONE IT STACKS, each frame at its own full width, because half of 390px
  is 195px and a garment at that size is a thumbnail. The reference is a desktop
  composition and is honest about it.

  The caption on each takes the frame's own measured polarity, exactly as any
  other label over photography does. Nothing new is measured and no scrim is
  introduced.
*/
import MediaSurface from "./MediaSurface.astro";
import type {MediaItem} from "../lib/media";
import type {Locale} from "../lib/locales";

/*
  A HALF MAY NOW BE A CUT-OUT (2026-08-13, section 118), and that is the
  reference's own version of this composition rather than a new idea.

  `RO04` is a 2-up whose left half is a bag against a body, cropped hard, and
  whose right half is A SINGLE SHOE ON PALE GROUND, small in the frame, with two
  thirds of that half left empty. Photograph against object; full bleed against
  air. It is the same device as two photographs and it reads differently.

  The cut-out half is `object-contain` on the page's own paper, inset, so the
  shape keeps its proportion and the emptiness around it is the composition. It
  is NOT cropped to fill: cropping a cut-out is how it stops being one.
*/
interface Props {
  locale: Locale;
  /** Exactly two. Fewer and this is not a split; more and it is a grid. */
  items: {media: MediaItem; label: string; href?: string | null; kind?: "frame" | "cutout"}[];
  /** The first screen after an arrival is worth fetching eagerly. */
  priority?: boolean;
  class?: string;
}

const {locale, items, priority = false, class: className = ""} = Astro.props;
const pair = items.slice(0, 2);
---

{
  pair.length === 2 && (
    <section class:list={["two-up", className]}>
      {pair.map(({media, label, href, kind = "frame"}) => {
        const frame =
          kind === "cutout" ? (
            <div class="two-up-cutout">
              <MediaSurface
                media={media}
                locale={locale}
                height="native"
                sizes="(min-width: 64rem) 34vw, 90vw"
                priority={priority}
              />
              <span class="type-label text-label">{label}</span>
            </div>
          ) : (
            <MediaSurface media={media} locale={locale} height="two-up" priority={priority}>
              <span class="type-label text-label">{label}</span>
            </MediaSurface>
          );
        return <div class="two-up-half">{href ? <a href={href} class="block">{frame}</a> : frame}</div>;
      })}
    </section>
  )
}
